1
00:00:00,106 --> 00:00:02,327
A casino does not know who will win tonight.

2
00:00:02,765 --> 00:00:07,752
But it knows, almost exactly, how much money it will make over a million games.

3
00:00:08,151 --> 00:00:12,260
A machine learning model does not know its error on the next example.

4
00:00:12,699 --> 00:00:15,718
But we can talk about its average error over all examples.

5
00:00:16,143 --> 00:00:19,773
That long-run average has a name: the expected value.

6
00:00:20,706 --> 00:00:30,865
In this video you will learn to compute an expected value, see why a fair die expects three point five, check a lottery, and discover a shortcut called linearity.

7
00:00:31,291 --> 00:00:35,520
Then we watch the average of real simulated rolls settle down.

8
00:00:35,945 --> 00:00:39,429
You need video twelve, the probability distribution.

9
00:00:39,842 --> 00:00:46,504
This atom is built from axiom A, linearity, and axiom D, the probability rules.

10
00:00:48,239 --> 00:00:50,659
Start with something familiar: a grade.

11
00:00:51,098 --> 00:00:55,327
Your homework score is eighty, and your exam score is ninety.

12
00:00:55,752 --> 00:00:57,135
But the exam counts more.

13
00:00:57,561 --> 00:01:01,989
Homework has a weight of thirty percent, the exam seventy percent.

14
00:01:02,415 --> 00:01:04,702
A plain average would give eighty five.

15
00:01:05,127 --> 00:01:06,870
That is not fair to the weights.

16
00:01:07,806 --> 00:01:11,223
Instead, multiply each score by its weight and add.

17
00:01:11,636 --> 00:01:14,960
Zero point three times eighty is twenty four.

18
00:01:15,372 --> 00:01:18,883
Zero point seven times ninety is sixty three.

19
00:01:19,309 --> 00:01:22,806
Twenty four plus sixty three is eighty seven.

20
00:01:23,245 --> 00:01:24,535
This is a weighted average.

21
00:01:24,973 --> 00:01:29,787
The weights add up to one, and big weights pull the result toward their score.

22
00:01:31,293 --> 00:01:32,290
Now the key idea.

23
00:01:32,729 --> 00:01:36,878
A probability distribution is a list of weights that add up to one.

24
00:01:37,317 --> 00:01:39,617
So we can use the probabilities as weights.

25
00:01:40,043 --> 00:01:43,859
Each value of the random variable is weighted by how likely it is.

26
00:01:44,285 --> 00:01:46,705
That weighted average is the expected value.

27
00:01:48,452 --> 00:01:49,955
Here is the formula.

28
00:01:50,394 --> 00:01:57,788
The expected value of X, written E of X, equals the sum over all values x of p of x times x.

29
00:01:58,280 --> 00:01:59,649
Read it piece by piece.

30
00:02:00,088 --> 00:02:01,710
The values x are what can happen.

31
00:02:02,149 --> 00:02:04,849
p of x is the weight of each value.

32
00:02:05,274 --> 00:02:07,468
Multiply, then add everything up.

33
00:02:09,059 --> 00:02:11,187
First example: one fair die.

34
00:02:11,626 --> 00:02:15,828
The values are one to six, each with probability one sixth.

35
00:02:16,253 --> 00:02:22,556
So E of X is one sixth times one, plus one sixth times two, and so on up to six.

36
00:02:22,982 --> 00:02:25,322
The sum of one to six is twenty one.

37
00:02:25,761 --> 00:02:28,727
Twenty one over six is three point five.

38
00:02:30,226 --> 00:02:31,622
Notice something strange.

39
00:02:32,061 --> 00:02:34,308
You can never roll three point five.

40
00:02:34,747 --> 00:02:37,952
The expected value does not have to be a possible value.

41
00:02:38,377 --> 00:02:39,375
It is a balance point.

42
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:46,516
If the bars of the distribution were weights on a ruler, the ruler would balance exactly at three point five.

43
00:02:48,039 --> 00:02:50,273
Second example: a toy lottery.

44
00:02:50,699 --> 00:02:52,534
A ticket costs two euros.

45
00:02:52,973 --> 00:02:57,946
With probability zero point one you win ten euros, so your net win is eight.

46
00:02:58,372 --> 00:03:03,412
With probability zero point nine you win nothing, so your net win is minus two.

47
00:03:03,837 --> 00:03:09,622
E of the win is zero point one times eight, plus zero point nine times minus two.

48
00:03:10,539 --> 00:03:15,672
That is zero point eight minus one point eight, which is minus one euro.

49
00:03:16,071 --> 00:03:19,183
On average, every ticket loses one euro.

50
00:03:19,608 --> 00:03:21,244
One single ticket can still win.

51
00:03:21,656 --> 00:03:25,393
But over many tickets, you lose about one euro per ticket.

52
00:03:25,818 --> 00:03:30,646
The expected value tells you the long-run truth, not the result of one game.

53
00:03:32,373 --> 00:03:33,862
Now a powerful property.

54
00:03:34,287 --> 00:03:36,362
The expected value is linear.

55
00:03:36,801 --> 00:03:41,960
E of a X plus b Y equals a times E of X, plus b times E of Y.

56
00:03:42,412 --> 00:03:45,258
Here a and b are ordinary numbers.

57
00:03:45,697 --> 00:03:52,359
This is axiom A again: the expected value respects adding and scaling, just like a linear mapping.

58
00:03:53,873 --> 00:03:54,631
Why is it true?

59
00:03:55,069 --> 00:03:58,327
E of X is a sum of probabilities times values.

60
00:03:58,753 --> 00:04:02,596
Scaling every value by a scales the whole sum by a.

61
00:04:03,021 --> 00:04:07,011
And the expected value of a sum splits into two sums.

62
00:04:07,450 --> 00:04:10,681
The sum rule from video twelve does the bookkeeping.

63
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:15,468
Linearity holds even when X and Y are not independent.

64
00:04:16,986 --> 00:04:18,462
Here is the shortcut.

65
00:04:18,887 --> 00:04:19,885
Roll two dice.

66
00:04:20,324 --> 00:04:21,441
What is the expected sum?

67
00:04:21,866 --> 00:04:24,765
With the table, you would add thirty six products.

68
00:04:25,191 --> 00:04:30,058
With linearity, E of X plus Y is E of X plus E of Y.

69
00:04:30,496 --> 00:04:34,406
That is three point five plus three point five, which is seven.

70
00:04:34,832 --> 00:04:35,709
No table needed.

71
00:04:37,239 --> 00:04:38,862
One more example with numbers.

72
00:04:39,287 --> 00:04:43,702
Take a die X, and make a new score: two times X plus one.

73
00:04:44,141 --> 00:04:49,620
Its expected value is two times three point five, plus one, which is eight.

74
00:04:50,058 --> 00:04:53,901
Be careful: linearity works for adding and scaling.

75
00:04:54,393 --> 00:05:01,588
For a curved function, like X squared, the expected value of X squared is not E of X, squared.

76
00:05:03,306 --> 00:05:05,593
What does long-run average mean in practice?

77
00:05:06,005 --> 00:05:11,178
Let's roll a die many times in a computer, with a fixed seed, and keep a running average.

78
00:05:11,604 --> 00:05:16,032
The first ten rolls are five, one, two, two, two, five, six, four, one, one.

79
00:05:16,457 --> 00:05:18,838
Their average is only two point nine.

80
00:05:20,373 --> 00:05:21,024
Keep rolling.

81
00:05:21,463 --> 00:05:22,766
Watch the running average move.

82
00:05:23,205 --> 00:05:26,011
After one hundred rolls it is three point four six.

83
00:05:26,450 --> 00:05:29,548
After one thousand, three point four five three.

84
00:05:29,987 --> 00:05:33,631
After ten thousand, three point four eight eight three.

85
00:05:34,056 --> 00:05:39,136
The wild jumps at the start calm down, and the line stays close to three point five.

86
00:05:40,673 --> 00:05:42,734
This is the law of large numbers.

87
00:05:43,173 --> 00:05:49,715
The average of many independent samples gets close to the expected value, as the number of samples grows.

88
00:05:50,154 --> 00:05:52,175
It never promises an exact match.

89
00:05:52,614 --> 00:05:55,313
It promises that big errors become rare.

90
00:05:55,739 --> 00:06:00,446
This is why a casino can plan, and why averages over data are reliable.

91
00:06:02,173 --> 00:06:06,508
Now look at a data set: two, four, four, five, ten.

92
00:06:06,946 --> 00:06:08,994
Its mean is the sum divided by the count.

93
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:11,814
Twenty five over five is five.

94
00:06:12,252 --> 00:06:15,165
The mean of a data set is the everyday average.

95
00:06:16,119 --> 00:06:17,489
Here is the connection.

96
00:06:17,941 --> 00:06:22,874
Pick one row of the data at random, each row with probability one fifth.

97
00:06:23,313 --> 00:06:25,055
That defines a random variable.

98
00:06:25,494 --> 00:06:31,319
Its expected value is one fifth times each value, added up, which is again five.

99
00:06:31,731 --> 00:06:37,010
So the mean of a data set is an expected value, under the pick a row at random distribution.

100
00:06:37,939 --> 00:06:41,782
The value four appears twice, so it gets the weight two fifths.

101
00:06:42,208 --> 00:06:46,849
Big values pull the mean up: the ten moves it more than any other row.

102
00:06:47,274 --> 00:06:55,851
In machine learning, we often write E of X for the true long-run average, and x bar for the mean of our samples, our best estimate of it.

103
00:06:57,559 --> 00:07:00,206
Now the most important use in machine learning.

104
00:07:00,711 --> 00:07:06,402
For every training example, the model makes an error, measured by a number called the loss.

105
00:07:06,828 --> 00:07:09,448
We will study losses in video fifteen.

106
00:07:09,860 --> 00:07:12,200
Training does not try to fix one example.

107
00:07:12,639 --> 00:07:15,977
It tries to make the average loss over all examples small.

108
00:07:17,493 --> 00:07:24,634
Take four toy examples with losses zero point two, zero point five, zero point one, and one point two.

109
00:07:25,059 --> 00:07:29,115
The average loss is two over four, which is zero point five.

110
00:07:29,554 --> 00:07:33,889
This average is an expected value under the pick a row at random distribution.

111
00:07:34,314 --> 00:07:37,479
It is our estimate of the expected loss on new data.

112
00:07:39,006 --> 00:07:48,607
And because expectation is linear, we can split the average loss into pieces, estimate it from small random batches, and take derivatives inside the sum.

113
00:07:49,032 --> 00:07:56,971
Most of modern training is: estimate an expected value from samples, then improve the model to make it smaller.

114
00:07:58,706 --> 00:07:59,251
Your turn.

115
00:07:59,650 --> 00:08:10,953
A random variable takes the value zero with probability zero point five, the value two with probability zero point three, and the value ten with probability zero point two.

116
00:08:11,392 --> 00:08:12,735
What is its expected value?

117
00:08:13,174 --> 00:08:13,879
Pause and try.

118
00:08:14,806 --> 00:08:15,923
Multiply and add.

119
00:08:16,362 --> 00:08:19,354
Zero point five times zero is zero.

120
00:08:19,793 --> 00:08:23,330
Zero point three times two is zero point six.

121
00:08:23,755 --> 00:08:26,574
Zero point two times ten is two.

122
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:28,715
The sum is two point six.

123
00:08:29,141 --> 00:08:32,452
The rare value ten pulls the expected value up a lot.

124
00:08:33,973 --> 00:08:34,824
Second question.

125
00:08:35,249 --> 00:08:36,619
Roll three dice.

126
00:08:37,044 --> 00:08:38,281
What is the expected sum?

127
00:08:38,707 --> 00:08:43,201
And what is the expected value of three times one die, minus two?

128
00:08:43,627 --> 00:08:44,717
Use linearity.

129
00:08:45,143 --> 00:08:45,848
Pause and try.

130
00:08:46,846 --> 00:08:54,505
For three dice, add three expected values: three point five, three times, which is ten point five.

131
00:08:54,971 --> 00:09:05,582
For three times X minus two, multiply and shift: three times three point five is ten point five, minus two is eight point five.

132
00:09:07,306 --> 00:09:09,194
Let's compute it all in NumPy.

133
00:09:09,620 --> 00:09:13,250
We multiply each face by its probability and sum.

134
00:09:13,689 --> 00:09:15,298
We do the same for the lottery.

135
00:09:15,803 --> 00:09:19,035
For two dice, we average all thirty six sums.

136
00:09:19,473 --> 00:09:26,654
Then we roll one die ten thousand times with a fixed seed, and compute the running average with a cumulative sum.

137
00:09:28,173 --> 00:09:32,042
This output is real; the script ran while the video was made.

138
00:09:32,468 --> 00:09:37,707
The die gives three point five, the lottery minus one, and two dice seven.

139
00:09:38,159 --> 00:09:40,845
The first ten rolls average two point nine.

140
00:09:41,271 --> 00:09:48,226
Then the averages go to three point four six, three point four five three, and three point four eight eight three.

141
00:09:48,665 --> 00:09:50,606
The data mean is five.

142
00:09:52,339 --> 00:09:53,004
In summary.

143
00:09:53,430 --> 00:10:02,180
The expected value is a weighted average of the values, with the probabilities as weights: E of X equals the sum of p of x times x.

144
00:10:02,618 --> 00:10:07,206
A fair die expects three point five, a value you can never roll.

145
00:10:07,632 --> 00:10:13,230
It is linear: E of a X plus b Y equals a E of X plus b E of Y.

146
00:10:14,173 --> 00:10:19,837
The average of many samples approaches the expected value: the law of large numbers.

147
00:10:20,276 --> 00:10:25,409
The mean of a data set is an expected value, and training minimizes an average loss.

148
00:10:25,835 --> 00:10:32,936
Next, in video fourteen: variance and covariance, how far values spread around this average.

149
00:10:34,873 --> 00:10:36,694
This video was made by David R.

150
00:10:37,133 --> 00:10:40,365
You can find more lessons at David Ramirez J dot com.

151
00:10:40,923 --> 00:10:41,641
Thanks for watching.
